ack` is set, `self.retry_error_callback` is added as an action and returns. In `iter`, `result = action(retry_state)` — the last action's result is returned from `iter`. So the callback's return value becomes the value returned by `iter`, and thus by `Retrying.__call__`. So YES — the callback's return value replaces raising RetryError. Let me check `Retrying.__call__` to confirm.\n\nLet me read the `Retrying` class `__call__` (line 531, offset 18427).",
